{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"30046a04070668d6","name":"card_event_costly_victory","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Japan/Breakthrough/events/card_event_costly_victory","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"COSTLY VICTORY","text":"Destroy target unit. Remove cards from the top of your deck equal to its attack.","flavorText":"During WWII the Japanese tactical doctrine was built on their inherent faith in superiority of the offense, even when defending they used every effort to regain the offensive and take initiative. The Japanese emphasized on high risk operations, they advanced fast, leaving their forces exhausted, thinly spread and insufficiently supplied. Also, while most military forces kept a reserve, the Japanese were willing to throw their reserve into battle. When this worked, it worked well, especially early in the war as their ferocious attacks unnerved the opponent, who either surrendered or retreated, leaving rations and equipment behind. As the Allied forces adjusted to these shock tactics and started to hold their ground, even in cases where they were surrounded,  the Japanese were much less likely to achieve their objectives and would often suffer terrible losses, not only in combat but also due to starvation and disease.","faction":"Japan","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":5,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_costly_victory","title":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_costly_victory_title","SourceString":"COSTLY VICTORY","LocalizedString":"COSTLY VICTORY"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Japan","kredits":5,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_costly_victory_text","SourceString":"Destroy target unit. Remove cards from the top of your deck equal to its attack.","LocalizedString":"Destroy target unit. Remove cards from the top of your deck equal to its attack."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_even